{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And the tree of life in the midst of the garden, and the tree of the knowledge of good and evil.</b> Since Scripture says \"the tree of life in the midst of the garden (בְּתוֹךְ הַגָּן)\" and does not say \"in the garden (בַּגָּן),\" and furthermore since it says (Genesis 3:3), \"but of the fruit of the tree which is in the midst of the garden (אֲשֶׁר בְּתוֹךְ הַגָּן), Elohim has said, You shall not eat of it\" — without identifying it or making it known by any other name — we shall say according to the plain sense that there was a known place in the garden that was in the middle, and therefore Onkelos rendered (Onkelos on Genesis 2:9) \"בִּמְצִיעוּת גִּנְּתָא\" (in the middle of the garden). Now according to his words, the tree of life and the tree of knowledge were both in the middle, and if so, we may say that it is as though a single bordered bed (עֲרוּגָה אַחַת סוּגָה) was made in the middle of the garden, and in it were these two trees, and this middle would be a broad middle, for regarding a narrow middle they have already said that no one knows its true point except HaShem alone.\n\nAnd the tree of life was a tree whose fruit grants long life to those who eat of it. \"And the tree of the knowledge of good and evil\" — the commentators said that its fruit would engender the desire for intercourse, and therefore after they ate of it they covered their nakedness, and they cited for it a parallel expression in the statement of Barzillai the Gileadite: \"Can I distinguish between good and evil?\" (II Samuel 19:36), for that desire had ceased from him. This is not correct in my eyes, because it says, \"and you shall be like Elohim, knowing good and evil\" (Genesis 3:5), and if you say he lied to her — behold, \"and YHWH Elohim said, Behold, the man has become like one of us, to know good and evil\" (Genesis 3:22), and they have already said (Pirka deRabbeinu HaKadosh, Bava d'Shaloshah 16) that three spoke truth and perished from the world, and these are: the serpent, the spies, and Doeg the Edomite the Beerothite.\n\nWhat seems correct to me is that man in his nature would do what is fitting to do according to the natural order, just as the heavens and all their host do — agents of truth whose activity is truth, and they do not change their function, and they have neither love nor hate in their actions. And the fruit of this tree would engender will and desire, so that those who ate of it would choose a thing or its opposite, for good or for evil; and therefore it was called \"the tree of the knowledge of good and evil,\" for \"knowledge (דַּעַת)\" in our language is said of will, as in their language (Pesahim 6): \"They changed nothing except that his mind (דַּעְתּוֹ) was to go back,\" and \"his mind (דַּעְתּוֹ) was to turn aside,\" and in the language of Scripture (Psalms 144:3), \"What is man that You should know him (תֵּדָעֵהוּ)\" — You desire and take pleasure in him; \"I have known you by name (יְדַעְתִּיךָ בְשֵׁם)\" (Exodus 33:12) — I have chosen you from among all men. And likewise the statement of Barzillai, \"Can I distinguish between good and evil?\" — for the power of intention had been lost from him, he would choose nothing and reject nothing, and he would eat without tasting and hear without delighting in song.\n\nNow at that time there was no desire for intercourse between a man and his wife, but at the time of procreation they would join and produce offspring, and therefore all the limbs were in their eyes like the face and the hands, and they would not be ashamed of them. But after he ate of the tree, the power of choice was in his hand — to do evil or good, whether for himself or for others — and this is a divine quality from one side, but evil for man when he possesses within it inclination and desire. And it is possible that Scripture intended this meaning when it said, \"Elohim made man upright, but they have sought out many inventions\" (Ecclesiastes 7:29) — the uprightness being that he would hold to one straight path, and the seeking out of many inventions being that he would seek for himself variable deeds through his own choice. And when the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded him concerning the tree not to eat of it, He did not inform him that this quality was in it, but said to him simply: \"but of the fruit of the tree which is in the midst of the garden (אֲשֶׁר בְּתוֹךְ הַגָּן)\" — that is, the one known by its centrality — \"you shall not eat of it,\" and this is the statement of the woman to the serpent. But the verse that says, \"but of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil you shall not eat\" (Genesis 2:17) — Scripture mentioned it there to us by its name."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:24:21.165990165Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}